      title: "Who Freed the Slaves?",
      body: `## The Great Emancipator

For a century after the war, the most common answer to the question "Who freed the slaves?" was "Abraham Lincoln." The image of the **Great Emancipator**, signing the proclamation that broke the chains of four million people, was enshrined in monuments, paintings, and schoolbooks. One of the most famous monuments, the **Freedmen's Memorial** (also known as the Emancipation Memorial) in Washington, dedicated in 1876 and paid for by donations from formerly enslaved people, shows Lincoln standing with the proclamation over a kneeling, shirtless Black man rising from his shackles. Frederick Douglass spoke at its dedication. Modern critics have objected to the monument's image of Black passivity; a copy was removed from Boston in 2020. Douglass himself, at the dedication, gave a more complicated verdict on Lincoln, as you will see at the end of this lesson.

## Self-emancipation

In the 1970s and 1980s, historians, led by the editors of the **Freedmen and Southern Society Project** at the University of Maryland—Ira Berlin, Barbara Fields, Leslie Rowland, and others—challenged this view. Drawing on thousands of documents in the National Archives, letters, petitions, and testimony by enslaved people, soldiers, and officials, they argued that the enslaved freed themselves. By fleeing to Union lines, by working for and fighting in the Union army, and by refusing to work for their masters, enslaved people forced the question of emancipation on a reluctant government, which followed rather than led. The enslaved, in their account, were the prime movers of emancipation. In *Slaves No More* (1992) and the multi-volume *Freedom: A Documentary History of Emancipation*, they showed emancipation as a process, not an event—a process that began with the first escapes in 1861 and continued long after the war.

The argument drew on an older tradition. In 1935, in *Black Reconstruction in America*, W. E. B. Du Bois had described the flight of the enslaved and their withdrawal of labor as a "**general strike**" against the Confederacy, which brought the slave system down from within.

## McPherson's reply

In an essay first delivered in 1995, **James McPherson** asked "Who Freed the Slaves?" He acknowledged the importance of the enslaved people's actions, but argued that the self-emancipation thesis went too far. Enslaved people could flee only where Union armies were present to receive and protect them; without Union military victory, the escapes of 1861–65 would have led only to recapture and punishment, as escapes had for centuries. It was Lincoln, McPherson argued, who decided that the Union army would become an army of liberation, who issued the Emancipation Proclamation, who refused to retract it even when it threatened his reelection, and who pushed the Thirteenth Amendment through Congress. Had a Democrat been elected in 1864, the war might have ended with slavery intact in much of the South. "Lincoln freed the slaves," McPherson concluded, though not by himself.

## A collaborative answer

Most historians today see emancipation as the product of many actors. The enslaved forced the issue by fleeing, working, and fighting. Union soldiers, many of whom came to hate slavery, carried freedom with them. Abolitionists, Black and white, kept up the pressure. Congress passed the Confiscation Acts and the Thirteenth Amendment. And Lincoln, whose decisions were essential at every stage, moved from a war for Union to a war for freedom, at a pace that frustrated abolitionists but carried most of the Northern public with him.

## The Sea Islands and land

Emancipation also raised the question of what freedom would mean. For many freed people, freedom meant land: the chance to work for themselves, on their own farms, free from white supervision. On the South Carolina Sea Islands, freed people bought some of the land sold for unpaid federal taxes, but most of it went to Northern investors.

In January 1865, in Savannah, General Sherman and Secretary of War Edwin Stanton met with twenty Black ministers and community leaders and asked them what they wanted. Their spokesman, **Garrison Frazier**, a sixty-seven-year-old Baptist minister who had bought his freedom, answered: "The way we can best take care of ourselves is to have land, and turn it and till it by our own labor." Four days later, Sherman issued **Special Field Order No. 15**, which set aside the Sea Islands and a strip of coastal land from Charleston to northern Florida for the exclusive settlement of freed families, each to receive up to forty acres. Some 40,000 freed people settled on about 400,000 acres. The order, along with the later practice of lending army mules, gave rise to the phrase "**forty acres and a mule**." As you will see in Lesson IV, President Andrew Johnson would reverse it.

## A nation under their feet

The historian **Steven Hahn**, in *A Nation Under Our Feet* (2003), argued that the enslaved had long developed their own political networks and traditions, which they mobilized during the war, in the process of emancipation, and in Reconstruction. Emancipation, in his account, was not something done for or to the enslaved, but a political struggle in which they were central actors. The rumors that spread through the quarters, the networks of kin and church, and the decisions of countless individuals to run, to stay, to fight, or to wait, together made emancipation possible. The historian Chandra Manning, in *Troubled Refuge* (2016), studied the contraband camps and found that freed people there forged an alliance of necessity with the Union army: the army needed their labor and knowledge, and they needed its protection, and out of that bargain came claims to rights that would shape the politics of Reconstruction. Emancipation, in this view, was negotiated on the ground, camp by camp and county by county.

> **Pause and consider.** "Who freed the slaves?" Is the question itself a useful one? What does each answer emphasize, and what does it obscure?`,
